+# Code review — September 29, 2026
+
+## What was checked
+
+- Parsed every Python source file with `py_compile`.
+- Installed the declared Discord dependencies and ran a non-Discord game smoke test against a temporary SQLite database.
+- Tested catalog/story synchronization, a pull, ownership lookup, 3-hero battle simulation, and all three authored story encounters.
+- Added and ran regression tests for party ownership and party placement.
+
+### Result
+
+The source parses successfully and the core game smoke test passed:
+
+- 15 catalog heroes synchronized
+- 12 heroes in the default banner pool
+- 3 story chapters loaded and simulated
+- Pull, battle, and owned-hero lookup flows completed without errors
+
+## Improvements included in this working copy
+
+### 1. Prevented party-instance cloning and cross-account placements
+
+`database.set_party_slot()` now:
+
+- verifies that the selected `user_warriors.id` belongs to the player;
+- returns `False` rather than attaching another player's character to a party;
+- moves an existing character instance to its new slot instead of allowing the same exact copy in several slots;
+- removes old duplicate placements left by previous versions.
+
+Different copies of the same catalog hero remain valid party members, because each owned copy has its own `user_warriors.id`.
+
+The two UI callers now show an appropriate refresh message if a stale menu selection refers to a character that no longer exists.
+
+### 2. Added regression tests
+
+`tests/test_database_party.py` verifies:
+
+- an owned warrior can be fetched only by its owner;
+- moving a character changes its slot rather than cloning it;
+- a player cannot place another player's character in their party.
+
+Run them with:
+
+```bash
+python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
+```
+
+### 3. Added onboarding and secret-safety files
+
+- Replaced the one-line `README.md` with setup, environment, command, testing, and data-safety instructions.
+- Added `.env.example` with safe placeholder values.
+- Updated `.gitignore` so `.env.example` is not accidentally ignored.
+
+## Important actions to take now
+
+### Remove `gacha.db` from the public repository
+
+The public repository currently tracks `gacha.db`. It has player IDs, display names, inventory, parties, and progression data. It does not appear to be a bot-token exposure, but it is live player data and should not be public.
+
+1. Keep your local working `gacha.db` file — that is the game data your bot uses.
+2. Delete `gacha.db` from GitHub's repository file list.
+3. Because it was included in the upload commit, simply deleting it does **not** remove it from Git history. Since this is a newly created repository, the simplest privacy-clean option is to create a fresh repository without `gacha.db`, or rewrite the repository history before sharing it further.
+4. Do not manually upload `gacha.db` in the GitHub web uploader again. `.gitignore` does not stop files that have already been tracked, and browser uploads require you to avoid selecting ignored files yourself.
+
+## Recommended next improvements
+
+### High priority: make economy-changing actions transactional
+
+A pull currently changes the Charm balance, adds each hero, updates pity, and records daily progress in separate database commits. If the bot process or database fails midway through a 10-pull, the account can be charged while receiving only part of the result, or pity can be incorrect.
+
+Create database-layer transaction functions for these multi-step actions:
+
+- pull(s): deduct Charms + add copies + update pity + record task progress;
+- shop purchases: deduct Solite + add Charms/Memory Stones/avatar;
+- daily claim: mark claim + grant Solite;
+- story win: grant all rewards + advance chapter + update stage.
+
+SQLite transactions make each action succeed entirely or not at all.
+
+### Medium priority: move startup work from `on_ready()` to `setup_hook()`
+
+`on_ready()` can run again after a reconnect. The bot currently runs database initialization, content synchronization, and global slash-command synchronization there. Repeated global command syncs can be slow or rate-limited.
+
+Keep logging in `on_ready()`, but perform one-time startup work in `setup_hook()` and guard any reconnect-sensitive work.
+
+### Medium priority: keep synchronous SQLite work off Discord's event loop as usage grows
+
+The current database is small and responds quickly, but most SQLite calls run directly inside async interaction handlers. With more players or slower hosting storage, interaction latency can increase. The bot already uses `asyncio.to_thread()` well for image work and battle simulation; the same approach or an async database layer can be applied to database-heavy screens and transactions.
+
+### Low priority: linter/type cleanup
+
+`ruff` reports 38 mostly non-blocking style/type-maintenance findings (unused imports, import ordering, overly broad exception handlers, and similar cleanup). The game smoke test passed, so these are not launch blockers. Address them gradually; do not blindly auto-fix broad exception handling without adding useful logging.
